    "root": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0000",
    "text": "**Status**: both directions are **implemented and live-verified** — `kg:path` (id→path, §8) and\n`kg:resolve` (path→id, §1-7, including `--base`, `--create-holder`, and the `kg:tree`/`kg:search`\nbackport in §7.5) — via `nodeRef.ts`'s `resolveNodeRefDetail`/`resolveNodeRefOrNull`."
  },
  {
    "@id": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJHG4G000",
    "@type": "ArtifactNode",
    "artifactId": "00C7BJHG4G000",
    "fileHash": "479e166df766343af34b10a587495342f1df1e692268105758226434a990ae2f",
    "lastTrackedAt": "2026-08-31T23:01:39.986Z",
    "path": "Aperas-dev-status.md",
    "title": "Aperas-dev-status.md",
    "ingestedHash": "479e166df766343af34b10a587495342f1df1e692268105758226434a990ae2f",
    "lastIngestedAt": "2026-08-31T23:02:37.541Z",
    "root": "BlockNode/00C7BJZHS0000",
    "text": "- ## Aperas Development Status\n\t- See `Aperas-architecture.md` for how everything below is actually built (schema, CRUD, WOQL/GraphQL, version control, artifact ingestion, KG backup/restore, cross-machine remotes).\n\t- Phase 0: Substrate and Core Skills — **Done**. Every roadmap item (`Aperas-design.md`'s Development Roadmap, Phase 0) is implemented and live-verified: direct database integration, `npm run verify:phase0 -- --db` + `npm run build` both clean, basic assertion authoring, and agentic query tools. The two loose ends open as of the last full review — cross-machine push/pull/rebase reconciliation, and Stage 2 item 5's title/text split — are both closed below. \"Not yet started\" (further down) carries nothing Phase-0-scoped anymore, only Phase 1/2 items.\n\t\t- Schema initialization — done.\n\t\t- Artifact tracking & on-demand ingestion (`kg:track`/`kg:ingest`) — done, re-verified live against the new fractal-ontology schema.\n\t\t- **Fixed**: `kg:ingest` used to double-ingest `README.md`. `listArtifactFiles` (`artifacts.ts`) walked every `.md` file with no README exclusion, so `ingestAllArtifacts` ingested a directory's `README.md` as an ordinary `ArtifactNode`+`BlockNode` tree — in addition to, and completely independently of, `folders.ts`'s `buildFolderTree`, which separately parses the same file and correctly folds its content into the owning `FolderNode`. Reproduced live before fixing: a real `README.md` produced both a correct `FolderNode`-absorbed copy and a separate stray `ArtifactNode`. Root cause: `artifacts.ts` and `folders.ts` each walked `AperasKG/artifacts/` with their own file-listing logic, only one of which knew to skip README files. Fixed by factoring one shared `isReadmeFilename` predicate into `artifacts.ts` (which `folders.ts` now imports instead of its own local constant) and applying it in `listArtifactFiles` — plus the same guard added to `kg:track`'s explicit single-file path (`kgCli.ts`), which bypasses that list entirely and needed its own check.\n\t\t- Automated tracking trigger (`AperasKG/.githooks/post-commit`, `post-index-change`) — done: `post-commit` auto-runs a scoped `kg:track` on commit, `post-index-change` auto-runs a full `kg:track` sweep on branch checkout / `git reset` (including `--hard`, which fires no hook of its own) — `post-checkout` was tried too but dropped as redundant once `post-index-change` was confirmed to fire alongside it with equivalent coverage.\n\t\t- Temporal commit management — done (`versionControl.ts` is class-agnostic — branch/commit/diff/reset operations, unaffected by the schema redesign below).\n\t\t- Cross-machine remotes — **done**: reciprocal SSH-tunneled TerminusDB remotes (reciprocal remote endpoints), `fetch` confirmed both directions. `push`/`pull` themselves only fast-forward and fail with `no_common_history` once both sides have diverged past their common point (no `--force` flag exists either) — reconciliation is `rebase` (replays one side's divergent commits onto a base built from the other, see `tdb-cli-status-walkthrough.md` §6) or, for a deliberate discard-and-replace sync, `reset` directly onto a fetched commit id (§7, reciprocal in both directions, no intermediate branch needed). Both live-verified against the real `aperas` DB: rebased a 9-commit remote-derived branch onto local `main`'s 1120 commits (zero conflicts; the reverse direction hangs — direction matters, see §6.2), then promoted `main` to the reconciled 1129-commit tip via `reset`.\n\t\t- Reference tooling — `skills/terminusdb/references/cli.md` audited end-to-end against a live server and the official CLI docs.\n\t- KG backup/restore — `restore.sh`/`tdb-log.sh`/`tdb-doc.sh` relocated to `Aperas/scripts/` and re-scoped as Core-Engine infra (documented in `Aperas-architecture.md` §1/§8), not domain-KG-specific — `db/` itself is gone from `AperasKG/`. `tdb-cross-diff.sh` was *not* migrated with them (its own hash-gated rewrite was already pending the ID-identity fix); genuinely gone, not relocated.\n\t- **RESOLVED** Design issue: \"AperasKG stores the KG\" was answered with a DB backup, not a KG export — corrected\n\t\t- `AperasKG` was designed to hold both the domain artifacts and the KG itself, but the KG lives in a DB — so the earlier approach to fulfilling that second half was to associate whole-TerminusDB-volume/bundle backups (`restore.sh`, briefly stored under `AperasKG/db/`) with the domain-KG repo. That coupling was the wrong move: a git-tracked repo has no good way to diff, merge, or meaningfully version an opaque binary DB snapshot, and (per the `#2509` investigation above) those snapshots turned out not to even be portable between independent TerminusDB stores — so it wasn't a viable backup path for `AperasKG` even setting the git-coupling problem aside.\n\t\t- Corrected via the JSON-LD import/export round-trip (`web/src/lib/export.ts`, `kg:export`/`kg:import`, `Aperas-architecture.md` §5): `AperasKG/Apeiron/` holds the canonical graph content as plain, diffable JSON-LD text, committed to the same repo as `AperasKG/artifacts/` — an ordinary `git commit` over that directory is now the KG's backup, `kg:import` is the restore path.\n\t\t- `restore.sh`'s whole-volume/bundle mechanism (§6) is unaffected and stays as-is — it's TerminusDB server infrastructure (Docker-volume disaster recovery), a different, unrelated concern from how `AperasKG` records its own content. Likewise, the SSH-tunneled TerminusDB remotes (§7) are unrelated to `AperasKG` — that's cross-machine sync for the TerminusDB server itself, not something this redesign touches.\n\t- **Fixed**: TerminusDB database renamed `aperas_apeiron` → `aperas` — the `aperas_` prefix was project-namespacing for a database name that already only ever means one thing on this dedicated instance, and `Apeiron` names a philosophical/architectural role (the fluid core substrate), not a resource identifier; naming the database after the project itself was simpler and avoided that mismatch. Migrated live via the JSON-LD round-trip rather than any TerminusDB-native rename (there isn't one — only `createDatabase`/`deleteDatabase`): `kg:export` from the old database, `kg:import` into a freshly created `aperas`, content parity verified class-by-class via the same order-independent `hashDocSet` hash `kg:import`'s own skip-if-unchanged check uses (all 5 matched: schema, `ArtifactNode`, `BlockNode`, `FolderNode`, `Assertion`), then the old `aperas_apeiron` database deleted. All defaults (`client.ts`, `tdb-log.sh`, `tdb-doc.sh`, `restore.sh`) and doc mentions updated to match.\n\t- **Fixed**: full migration to the fractal ontology, `FolderNode` implemented, `npm run verify:phase0 -- --db` and `npm run build` both passing (see \"RESOLVED\" entries below for detail).\n\t- **Fixed**: `ArtifactNode` missing `@inherits: [\"BaseNode\"]` in `schema.json` — added, live and verified (`kg:track`/`kg:ingest` run cleanly against it).\n\t- **RESOLVED** Design issue: Positional identity replaced by Fractal Ontology\n\t\t- The legacy flat `DocumentNode` / `BlockNode` / `SpanNode` schema was entirely eliminated.\n\t\t- Designed and implemented a universal, recursive `BlockNode` topology (Fractal Ontology) rooted in `ArtifactNode`.\n\t\t- Schema updated to pure JSON-LD (`schema.json`), avoiding TypeScript interface duplication.\n\t\t- IDs & content fingerprint — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1). `web/src/lib/snowflake.ts` generates the 64-bit Snowflake-style identifier (timestamp + machine identity w/ personal failover + sequence counter, 13-char Crockford Base32); `astParser.ts` uses it for every `blockId` instead of the old content hash. `ArtifactNode.contentHash` renamed to `fileHash` throughout (`artifacts.ts`, `schema.json`) to match §1.B's file-domain-only fingerprint — no structural/recursive hash. Verified live: cleared stale pre-redesign `ArtifactNode`/`BlockNode` docs, ran `kg:track`+`kg:ingest` across all 8 artifacts (1144 blocks total, including a 648-block single-artifact burst), zero id collisions. Also found and fixed a real pre-existing bug while verifying: `getArtifactRecord` treated a missing document's raw 404 response (a string, not a thrown error) as a valid record, so untracked artifacts silently reported \"unchanged\" instead of erroring. Reconciliation (best-effort content matching, §1.C history in Appendix F) remains unimplemented — no code needed it yet, since ingestion so far has only ever run against uningested artifacts.\n\t\t- Ingestion (`astParser.ts` and `artifacts.ts`) now parses Markdown into a deeply nested tree structure and commits the entire fractal tree via `ArtifactNode.root` in a single TerminusDB operation. Note: a plain `getDocument` read only ever returns `root`/`children` as reference ids, not the nested tree, so full-tree reads go through `getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` (`graphql.ts`) instead — deliberately, not because the Document API is incapable of it (see `Aperas-architecture.md` §2's \"Read-path note\" for the full investigation, including a filed upstream bug, [terminusdb/terminusdb#2512](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2512)).\n\t\t- `FolderNode` — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §4.A). `web/src/lib/folders.ts` walks `AperasKG/artifacts/`, builds one `FolderNode` per directory, absorbs each directory's own `README.md` (parsed blocks become the folder's children; the file itself is never exposed as a separate `ArtifactNode`), and references nested `FolderNode`s/`ArtifactNode`s by id. Wired into `npm run kg:ingest`, which now rebuilds the whole folder tree in the same run as artifact ingestion. Added one concrete `Assertion` class (inherits the previously-uninstantiable abstract `BaseEdge`) to `schema.json`, needed to let `crud.ts`/`woql.ts` actually write/query extrinsic edges — documented in `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.D. `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` are now Snowflake-keyed (`folderId`/`artifactId`), `path` a plain mutable field — see the \"Reconciliation matching\" entry below for the full migration.\n\t\t- `crud.ts`/`woql.ts`/`graphql.ts`/`verifyPhase0.ts` — **migrated off the dead pre-redesign schema and live-verified**. `crud.ts` now provides `insertAssertion`/`deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` for the new `Assertion` type; `woql.ts` queries `Assertion.source`/`predicate`/`target` (node-typed, not the old flat `xsd:string` `subjectId`/`objectId`) in both directions plus one-hop impact propagation; `graphql.ts` replaced the removed `docId`-scoped block query with `getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` (bounded-depth nested `root`/`children` selection — GraphQL has no native recursion, so depth is generated explicitly, default 10). `verifyPhase0.ts` was rewritten into a full idempotent, self-cleaning live test of the whole pipeline (AST parsing → track/ingest → GraphQL tree read → `FolderNode` ingestion → `Assertion` CRUD + WOQL traversal → temporal commit management); `npm run verify:phase0 -- --db` and `npm run build` both confirmed passing.\n\t\t- **Discovered while wiring this up**: TerminusDB enforces referential integrity on document delete — deleting a still-referenced document (e.g. an `ArtifactNode` still listed in a `FolderNode.children`, or a `BlockNode` still pointed to by `ArtifactNode.root`) fails with a `SchemaCheckFailure`, not silently. The best-effort delete helpers in `crud.ts` (`deleteDocumentIfExists`/`deleteDocumentsIfExist`) used to swallow *all* errors on the assumption only \"not found\" could occur — that assumption was wrong and masked this failure mode; they now log instead of swallowing silently, and any caller managing referenced state (like `verifyPhase0.ts`'s cleanup) has to delete in reverse-reference order.\n\t\t- **Heading levels now define tree structure** — **implemented and live-verified**. `astParser.ts` gained `groupByHeadings`, a one-pass depth-stack transform run on every flat mdast sibling list before conversion: each heading becomes the parent of everything until a heading of depth <= its own. A doc with `# Title` / `## Section A` / `## Section B` now parses as nested (`Title` containing `Section A`, `Section B`, each with their own body), not a flat list under the root. Alongside this: every `BlockNode` now carries its raw mdast `type` (needed by reconciliation's leaf/container split below), and `list`/`listItem`/`blockquote` no longer carry the full-subtree-duplicate `text` they used to (confirmed live pre-fix: a `listItem`'s `text` byte-for-byte duplicated its own paragraph child's) — containers now carry no content of their own, by design.\n\t\t- **Reconciliation matching for re-ingestion** — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-reconciliation-matching-design.md`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1.B/§4.A/§4.B/Appendix G). New `web/src/lib/reconcile.ts`: a Gestalt (Ratcliff/Obershelp) tree diff — the longest-common-run algorithm behind Python's `difflib` — applied two-stage per level (Stage A anchors on leaf content by `heading XOR text`; Stage B aligns containers by type within the segments Stage A's anchors define), with ambiguous same-content duplicates declined rather than guessed. Matched `BlockNode`s reuse their old `blockId`; unmatched old ones get an explicit `tombstonedAt` (new field on `BaseNode`, inherited everywhere) rather than being silently orphaned; a block relocated to a different section is detected as a cross-parent move via the same mechanism, one level up from per-parent matching. `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` moved off `Lexical`-on-`path` to Snowflake ids (`artifactId`/`folderId`, same scheme as `BlockNode.blockId`), `path` now a plain mutable field with `title`/`text` added to `ArtifactNode` (filename / naive first-paragraph abstract, matching `FolderNode`'s existing shape) — this is what makes a file/folder rename a field update instead of a delete-and-recreate. Rename detection reuses the identical Gestalt-matching primitive one level up: on every `kg:track`/`kg:ingest` sweep, disk-only and DB-only leftover paths are matched by abstract-text similarity (`matchLeftoverByAbstract`) before falling back to plain add/remove. Wired into `ingestArtifact` (fires whenever a prior ingestion exists), `trackAllArtifacts`, and `ingestFolderTree`; `kgCli.ts` reports renamed/removed/reconciled counts alongside the existing tracked/ingested counts. Live-verified via `verifyPhase0.ts` (edit-and-reingest keeps unrelated `blockId`s stable, reports sane unchanged/added counts) and a manual rename test against a throwaway artifact (`artifactId` and `blockId`s survived a real on-disk move). Migrating the key scheme required clearing all pre-existing `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`/`BlockNode` documents first, since TerminusDB rejects a schema whose new required fields the old instance data doesn't have — the whole KG was then rebuilt from `AperasKG/artifacts/` via a full `kg:track` + `kg:ingest` sweep, per the project's disk-is-source-of-truth model.\n\t\t- **Artifact Projection** — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A mode 1 of 3). New `web/src/lib/project.ts` serializes an `ArtifactNode`'s ingested `BlockNode` tree back into Markdown — canonical regeneration, not byte-exact: headings/paragraphs/code round-trip via their raw `title`/`text`, while `list`/`blockquote` are regenerated as clean Markdown (list items always blank-line-separated regardless of original tight/loose style; blockquote `> ` re-prefixed uniformly rather than trusting what the raw text happens to carry). Needed two new captured fields on `BlockNode` — `ordered`/`start` (list numbering) and `checked` (task-list checkbox) — both `Optional`, so unlike the `artifactId`/`type` migration this needed no DB wipe. Exposed via `npm run kg:project -- <path>`. Verified two ways: `verifyPhase0.ts`'s synthetic sample (every branch: ordered/unordered/task lists, nested list, blockquote, code) round-trips with zero drift by reusing `reconcile.ts`'s own matcher as the equivalence check; and, more usefully, running the same check against all 11 real artifacts in `AperasKG/artifacts/` surfaced two genuine bugs no synthetic sample had exercised — a nested code fence's closing `` ``` `` desyncing from its opening one because only a multi-line leaf's *first* line has container-required indentation stripped (every later line keeps its literal original-file column; fixed with a `dedent` normalization), and an *indented* (4-space) code block silently degrading into an ordinary paragraph because its raw slice carries no fence to re-emit (fixed by re-fencing it). After those two fixes, 9 of 11 real artifacts round-trip with zero drift; the other 2 each show one accepted, disclosed difference (absolute code-indentation depth normalized to the minimum; indented-style converted to fenced-style) — real content changes under exact-key matching, not bugs, per the design doc §8.\n\t\t- **Basic Assertion Authoring** — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`). New `kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert` CLI commands (`kgCli.ts`) let a human or agent actually write and inspect a real `Assertion` — previously the only thing that ever called `insertAssertion` was a hardcoded synthetic edge in `verifyPhase0.ts`'s test harness. `source`/`target` accept either a full node id (direct addressing) or a bare artifact/folder path (query-based addressing, resolved via `getArtifactRecord`/the new `getFolderRecord`, artifact tried first since the two never collide) — block-level assertions stay direct-id-only; closing that gap became its own effort, see \"Agentic Query Tools\" below. New narrow `deleteAssertion` (`crud.ts`) does exact source/predicate/target deletion, distinct from the existing blunt `deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` (kept for demo/reset cleanup only). Live end-to-end test against two real artifacts (assert → list → unassert → list-empty) surfaced one real bug: WOQL binds a literal-typed field (`predicate`, `xsd:string`) as a wrapped `{\"@type\", \"@value\"}` object rather than a plain string, unlike node-typed `source`/`target` bindings — `kg:assertions` printed `[object Object]` until fixed with an `unwrapLiteral` helper in `woql.ts`'s `queryNodeAssertions`. Never caught by `verifyPhase0.ts`'s existing Assertion test because that only checks binding presence/membership, never a literal field's actual value. Reordered from Phase 2 into Phase 0 itself (not merely Phase 1) per `Aperas-design.md`'s roadmap, so Phase 1 skill-building starts with it already available.\n\t\t- **Agentic Query Tools** — reordered from Phase 1 into Phase 0, design settled (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md`) — **implemented and live-verified**. Three CLI tools closing the block-level addressing gap Basic Assertion Authoring (above) left open, and making mode 3 of `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A concrete for the first time: `kg:tree` (a deep structural map — depth-first with an optional depth bound; title-only by default), `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold` (the actual breadth-first primitive: one level, full content, the first code to ever give the long-inert `unfolded` flag a real effect), and `kg:search` (keyword/regex content search via `WOQL.re` — two unrestricted `title`/`text` queries rather than one per class, since the property names are shared across all three node classes and the id prefix alone already reveals kind). Smoke-tested against the real KG (`kg:tree`, `kg:search`, an `unfold`/`fold` round-trip on a real `BlockNode`, `kg:project`).\n\t\t  - **`unfolded` promoted from `BlockNode` to `BaseNode`** (§4.1) — the field had only ever lived on `BlockNode`, a leftover from before `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` existed as separate concrete classes rather than a deliberate restriction. `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold` now persist real state against any node kind, not just display normally with nothing to save for the other two. `kg:tree --unfolded` renders the *combined* shape a sequence of `kg:fold`/`kg:unfold` calls leaves behind in one walk instead of separate scrollback: a node prints full `text` instead of `title` exactly when its own parent has `unfolded === true`, recursion stops at a folded node (with a `(folded — kg:unfold <id> to expand)` hint naming the exact fix), and the walk's own root always stays title-only — composes with `--depth`/`--no-holders` unchanged.\n\t\t  - **Real bug found live during this promotion**: `trackArtifact`'s (`artifacts.ts`) and `buildFolderTree`'s (`folders.ts`) record-rebuild paths already carry forward fields like `text`/`root`/`ingestedHash` from the previous document on every re-track/re-ingest, but neither had ever needed to carry forward a *view-state* field before — `unfolded` would have been silently reset to folded on the very next `kg:track`/`kg:ingest` of an `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`, the same class of regression `reconcile.ts` was already fixed for on `BlockNode`. Fixed by adding `unfolded` to `normalizeArtifactDoc`'s carried-forward field set and threading `existingByPath` into `buildFolderTree` (which, unlike `ArtifactNode`, rebuilds its whole tree fresh from disk every run rather than reusing a stored record, so it has nothing to carry forward from without an explicit lookup). Confirmed live: unfolded an `ArtifactNode`, forced a real re-track (content hash change), and it stayed unfolded; same for a `FolderNode` across a direct `ingestFolderTree` re-run.\n\t\t- **Markdown → Fractal Tree Mapping** (`Aperas-markdown-fractal-mapping-design.md`) — **implemented and live-verified**: `heading`/`listItem` now *consume* (not copy) their leading paragraph as `text`, shallow, one node fewer per section; a `list` now dissolves into whichever `paragraph`/`listItem`/`heading` immediately precedes it (`orderedList`/`startIndex` becoming that adopting node's own `props`) rather than remaining a standalone node, orphaned only when nothing valid precedes it — including the case, found while implementing this, where two lists sit directly adjacent to each other (the second is orphaned, since a `list` itself is never a valid adoption anchor); a new generic `Prop`/`StringProp` mechanism (`BaseNode.props: Set<Prop>`, `props.ts`'s shared `getProp`/`setProp`) replaces the one-field-per-feature pattern, `orderedList`/`startIndex`/`checked` all migrated onto it; YAML frontmatter is extracted via `remark-frontmatter` and stored as one `props` entry on both `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` (`parseMarkdownTree` now returns `{root, frontmatter?}`); `folders.ts`'s README absorption got the same copy-to-consume fix. `project.ts`'s serializer was generalized to match: `heading`/`listItem` emit their consumed `text` before their children, and `renderChildren` scans for contiguous `listItem` runs anywhere in a node's children (not just a dedicated `list` case) using that node's own `props` for numbering. One real GraphQL wrinkle surfaced and fixed: `props: Set<Prop>` can't be queried with an inline fragment on `StringProp` (TerminusDB's generated GraphQL schema makes `Prop` its own concrete type, not an interface `StringProp` implements) — worked around via the generic `_json` field every generated type carries, parsed back into `{key, value}` by `graphql.ts`'s `normalizeProps`. `verify:phase0 -- --db` and `npm run build` both clean. Same known limitation as Artifact Projection/Agentic Query Tools above: already-ingested real artifacts won't reflect any of this until something forces their re-ingestion.\n\t- **Interactive Title & Cross-Link Authoring** (`Aperas-interactive-summarization-design.md`) — **implemented and live-verified**. Resolves Stage 2 item 5 below (`Aperas-fractal-ontology-implementation-plan.md`, \"AI-driven summarization... splitting blocks into `title` vs `text`\") — reframed per the project's own zero-background-API principle (`Aperas-design.md` Phase 3 rationale): no LLM call, the CLI just asks whoever is running it (human or an already-in-the-loop coding agent) directly. New `kg:title`/`kg:link` (`kgCli.ts`) prompt interactively for a real title on any still-unlabeled `BlockNode`, or cross-links a block's prose makes that wikilink-syntax extraction (`astParser.ts:92-106`) doesn't catch; both share a new `updateBlockNode` fetch-merge-replace primitive (`crud.ts`) since none existed before. A real regression this design surfaced and fixed before it could ship: `reconcileNode` (`reconcile.ts`) only carried `blockId` forward for a matched (content-unchanged) pair, so a title/link set today would've silently reverted on the artifact's next re-ingest — now carries `title`/`unfolded`/`links` forward too (`carryForwardFields`), and `resolveBlockLinks` (`artifacts.ts`) merges freshly-resolved wikilinks onto that carried-forward `links` instead of overwriting it. Two more bugs found live during this work, both fixed: `graphql.ts`'s block field selection never included `links` at all (so the carry-forward had nothing to carry — added a `links { _id }` selection plus a `normalizeLinks` step converting GraphQL's full-IRI `_id` to the short ref-id form `updateDocument` expects, mirroring the existing `_json`/`normalizeProps` pattern); and `rl.question()` (readline/promises) races against stream-end once real `await`s happen between prompts, which piped/non-interactive stdin (a coding agent driving the tool with pre-computed answers) hits — fixed by consuming the readline interface's own async iterator (`createLineReader`) instead. Live-verified end-to-end: a title and a cross-link set via these commands both survived a real re-ingestion round-trip, confirmed via direct `doc get`, not just the tools' own reported output.\n\t- **Deep Path Resolution** (`Aperas-deep-path-resolution-design.md`) — **implemented and live-verified**, both directions. `kg:path <id>` (id→path): a new `parent: Optional<BaseNode>` field on `BlockNode` (`astParser.ts`'s `stampParents`, re-run post-reconciliation since `carryForwardFields` reassigns `blockId`s), walked upward by `nodeRef.ts`'s `resolveIdToPath` to the owning `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`. `kg:resolve <path>` (path→id, the reverse): extends `nodeRef.ts`'s `resolveNodeRefOrNull` with the full deep-path grammar — `.`/`..`/leading `/` (Linux-flavored relative addressing, `--base <path>` for batch resolution), exact-then-prefix title matching with \"decline rather than guess\" on ambiguity, and `--create-holder` (a tombstone-like placeholder for a target that doesn't exist yet, tail-aligned `--titles` so a caller only supplies titles for segments actually being created) reaching all the way from a single heading up through imagining whole new `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` chains from nothing. Wired into `artifacts.ts`'s wikilink extractor (forward references to not-yet-written headings now resolve to a holder instead of being silently dropped) and backported into `kg:tree`/`kg:search` (a `(holder)` mark, `--no-holders` to filter — transparent for indentation in `kg:tree`, not a subtree prune, since a holder is per-node not per-branch). New `holder: Optional<xsd:boolean>` field on `BaseNode` (not a `Prop` — a first-class concept needed on every node kind, not parser-derived per-node metadata); `ArtifactNode.fileHash`/`lastTrackedAt` made `Optional` to let a holder `ArtifactNode` exist with no real file behind it yet.\n\n\t  Several real bugs found live during implementation, none of them the original design's own text (all consistent with what `Aperas-deep-path-resolution-design.md` itself now documents inline, §4/§7): a heading's leading `#`/`##` slugifying to leading dashes made prefix matching dead on arrival until the candidate's dashes were stripped before the `startsWith` check; writing a holder directly under an `ArtifactNode` failed schema check because its one child is the singular `root` field, not a `children` List; and, most fundamentally, resolving wikilinks *before* the tree's own write was a genuine chicken-and-egg bug (the implicit base needs an already-persisted `.parent` chain, so anything brand-new in the current edit would resolve against the *previous* ingestion's stale tree) — fixed by moving link resolution to a separate pass after the write, extracting/stripping each block's raw `linkCodes` beforehand since `schema.json` has no such field and leaving it in fails the write itself. `verifyPhase0.ts`'s wikilink test extended to cover all three shapes end-to-end (a bare snowflake code, a forward reference that becomes a real holder, and a still-genuinely-unresolvable one that correctly declines) rather than just the original single self-link case.\n\t- Not yet started\n\t\t- **Projection Engines (Phase 1)**: the Human UI (SolidJS read-projection, 'Hover Preview' link mechanism) remains undesigned — of the three modes in `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A, mode 1 (Artifact Projection, now covering both `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` — see \"Markdown → Fractal Tree Mapping\" above) is implemented and mode 3 (Agentic Interface) is designed (Phase 0, above); only mode 2 (Human UI) is still untouched. Mode 1 now also writes to disk by default (`kg:project`, `--dry-run` to print instead) rather than print-only, per `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1.B's \"the DB becomes the source of truth\" model.\n\t\t- **Assertion skill(s) (Phase 1)**: a loadable `SKILL.md` teaching a coding agent when/how to use the `kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert` CLI (built in Phase 0, above) — not yet designed.\n\t\t- Phase 2+ skills: ingestion-from-arbitrary-sources, *automated* graph search skill (the manual assertion primitive moved to Phase 0, see below), refactoring/lazy-atomization skill.\n\t- ## Appendix: cross-store `bundle`/`unbundle` incompatibility investigation\n\t\t- Historical record — the `restore.sh`/`bundle` workflow this investigation covers predates its relocation to `Aperas/scripts/` (see \"KG backup/restore\" above); kept for reference in case a similar mechanism is reintroduced.\n\t\t- Symptom: `aperas_apeiron_snapshot_A.bundle` (made on Host A) and `aperas_apeiron_snapshot_B.bundle` (made on Host B) each verify cleanly **only on their own origin machine**, and fail deterministically with `error(unknown_layer_reference(<hash>))` on the other — a different specific missing-layer hash each time.\n\t\t- An earlier pass at this investigation, run on a single host in isolation, found a byte pattern in a snapshot that looked like Latin-1/UTF-8 mojibake corruption (reversing it produced a plausible gzip header) and concluded the file was corrupted. **That conclusion was wrong and has been retracted** — the same file was later shown to unbundle perfectly on its own origin host, which a genuinely corrupted file cannot do. The byte pattern was a coincidental artifact, not evidence of corruption.\n\t\t- Ruled out version mismatch: both machines confirmed running byte-identical `TerminusDB v12.0.7 (57f2093baeafd65e16004e84b7b58e0c5cf72858)` / `terminusdb-store v0.19.8`.\n\t\t- Ruled out the `auto-optimize` community plugin (`/plugins/auto-optimize.pl`, a probabilistic post-commit layer squash/GC): disabled on both machines (moved the plugin file out, restarted), re-tested with a brand-new minimal scratch database — still failed cross-host identically.\n\t\t- Ruled out store \"age\": reproduced with a store `store init`'d seconds before the test, same as the destination.\n\t\t- **Decisive isolation, no second machine needed**: ran two independent TerminusDB containers (`terminusdb` and a throwaway `terminusdb-freshtest`, same image, same physical host, plugin off on both) and bundled/unbundled between them. Same failure. A positive control — unbundling the same file back into a *different database within its own originating container* — succeeded every time. This isolates the variable precisely: **it's not host A vs. host B, or even separate machines at all — it's any two independent TerminusDB stores (server instances/storage directories), even side-by-side on one host.**\n\t\t- Cross-checked against prior art: [terminusdb/terminusdb#1147](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/1147) reports the identical symptom moving a bundle between two instances, never reproduced by the maintainer, closed stale/not-planned. [terminusdb/terminusdb#1235](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/pull/1235) (\"an initial effort to figure out #1147 without success\") added CLI bundle/unbundle tests that call `store.init()` exactly once and share that one store across every test — so its test suite never actually exercises the cross-store case, which is the one that fails.\n\t\t- The official [\"move a database between servers\" backup/restore guide](https://terminusdb.org/docs/enterprise-backup-restore/) is scoped to **Enterprise edition** (different mechanism: `/api/bundle` + `/api/unbundle` REST endpoints, target pre-created) — not a documented guarantee for the Community-edition CLI commands we're using.\n\t\t- Filed as [terminusdb/terminusdb#2509](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2509), including the two-store repro and a note about the test-coverage gap in #1235. Draft/record kept at `terminusdb-issue-bundle-cross-store.md`.\n\t\t- Conclusion for this project: `bundle`/`unbundle`/`restore.sh` remain valid for same-store snapshot/rollback, but are not to be relied on for cross-machine transfer."
  },
